         <abstract>
            <p>This is a general record of any work teaching the Orthodox faith and concerning the five mysteries: the mystery of the Trinity, the Incarnation, the Baptism, the Eucharist, and the Resurrection of the Dead.
               At a cursory survey, it seems that the most widespread version is that contained in manuscripts such as <ref type="mss" corresp="ESddm013"/>, <ref type="mss" corresp="EMML379"/>, 
               <ref type="mss" corresp="EMML4404"/>, and <ref type="mss" corresp="EMML4963"/>.
               The texts vary in length, from very concise ones such as <ref type="mss" corresp="EMML3247"/> and <ref type="mss" corresp="EMML3517"/>, which contain only four folia, to those of considerable length, 
               with over one hundred folia, such as <ref type="mss" corresp="EMML3408"/>.
               The text in <ref type="mss" corresp="EMDA206"/>, dated to 1792, is atypical in that a relatively large part of the text consists of Gǝʿǝz quotations, whereas Amharic narration is present to a lesser extent.
               The text in <ref type="mss" corresp="EMML1288"/> is peculiar in that the mysteries of the faith are consistently presented in a dialogue of questions and answers, which is also reflected in the layout
               Some texts begin with an introduction, which may or may not be part of the ʾAmmǝstu ʾaʿmāda mǝśṭir. 
               In this introduction, the whole text is called “Maṣḥafa mǝgbār wa-hāymānot” (e.g., <ref type="mss" corresp="EMML 5048"/>, <ref type="mss" corresp="EMML5594"/>).
               In <ref type="mss" corresp="EMML5594"/>, Maṣḥafa mǝgbār wa-hāymānot is separated from the ʾAmmǝstu ʾaʿmāda mǝśṭir by another text.
            </p>
